Ctrl+K reaches the palette from inside the console already; this is the
same chord over a web page. It has to be intercepted before the renderer
rather than left to the unhandled-key path, because the sites the product
exists to automate - Slack, Linear, GitHub - bind Ctrl+K themselves and
would swallow it. That would leave the shortcut working everywhere except
where it is wanted.

The intercept only routes. Which surface the palette is, and how it opens,
is decided in FluxSidebarView::OpenCommandPalette so that this patch stays
a small intercept and the product decision stays in Flux's own source.

This does displace Chromium's own Ctrl+K, which focuses the omnibox in
search mode. Ctrl+E remains bound to that.

--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/frame/browser_view.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/frame/browser_view.cc
@@ -3194,6 +3194,23 @@
     return content::KeyboardEventProcessingResult::NOT_HANDLED;
   }
 
+  // Flux: Ctrl+K opens the console's command palette, and has to win over the
+  // page. Left to the unhandled-key path it would work everywhere except the
+  // sites worth automating - Slack, Linear and GitHub all bind Ctrl+K
+  // themselves - which is the wrong way round for a launcher.
+  //
+  // Compared field by field rather than against an Accelerator built here:
+  // Accelerator::operator== includes key_state_, and one built inline defaults
+  // to PRESSED, so it would match the key-down and let the key-up fall through
+  // to the page as a release for a chord it never received.
+  if (flux_sidebar_ && accelerator.key_code() == ui::VKEY_K &&
+      accelerator.modifiers() == ui::EF_PLATFORM_ACCELERATOR) {
+    if (event.GetType() == blink::WebInputEvent::Type::kRawKeyDown) {
+      flux_sidebar_->OpenCommandPalette();
+    }
+    return content::KeyboardEventProcessingResult::HANDLED;
+  }
+
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
